<description>
Repair the maroon-stained pattern by replacing each damaged pixel with the color forced by the pattern symmetries.

Input structure:
- A square grid contains one occluding color whose pixels are not part of the final design.
- Ignoring the occluding color reveals unique shifted horizontal and vertical reflection axes with the same center.
- Any occluding pixels left unresolved by reflection lie in edge bands that also obey the same-center quarter-turn relation.

Rule:
- Infer the occluding color as the only color whose removal gives conflict-free high-evidence row and column reflection axes.
- Fill occluding pixels from the unique visible color in their horizontal, vertical, and 180-degree reflection orbit.
- Fill any remaining occluding pixels from the unique visible color at a 90-degree quarter-turn counterpart.

Key insight:
- The maroon pixels are damage over a shifted kaleidoscope-like pattern; the hidden color is determined by a visible mate in the same symmetry orbit.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "maroon occlusion mask over a shifted two-axis reflection pattern with matching edge quarter-turn bands",
  "transform": "replace each maroon pixel with the unique non-maroon color from its horizontal/vertical reflection orbit; if that orbit is fully occluded or outside the shifted mirror domain, use the unique same-center quarter-turn counterpart",
  "multi_step": "infer the occluding color and shifted row/column axes from non-maroon consistency, then repair reflection orbits before quarter-turn edge leftovers"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 30x30 square [observed]
  colors:
    pattern: exactly colors 1-8
    hole: 9 (maroon), absent from output
  symmetry:
    type: shifted horizontal + vertical reflection
    axis_sum: 31 (axis at position 15.5, offset +1 from center)
    row_axis == col_axis (same center)
    coverage: entire output obeys both reflections perfectly
    edge_bands: rows 0-1 and cols 0-1 lack one reflection partner (mapped to OOB) but still obey the other axis
  occlusion:
    blob_count: 2-3 connected components (4-connected)
    total_pixels: 30-110 (3-12% of grid)
    blob_size: 4-56 pixels each
    blob_bbox: 2-8 rows x 2-11 cols
    shape: rectangular or L-shaped (union of 2 rectangles)
    placement: anywhere in grid, may touch boundary
  repairability:
    primary: every hole has >=1 non-hole reflection orbit mate (orbit = {(r,c), (A-r,c), (r,A-c), (A-r,A-c)} where A=31)
    fallback: holes in rows 0-1 whose V-partner is also a hole are repaired by quarter-turn (r,c)->(A-c,r)
    convergence: reflection fills all holes in 1 pass (no cascading)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Generate the symmetric output pattern on 30x30 grid using colors 1-8.
       Free domain = rows 0-15, cols 0-15 (256 cells). Fill randomly (with moderate spatial correlation [observed]).
       Reflect to fill rows 16-29 via out[A-r,c]=out[r,c] for r in 2-15.
       Reflect to fill cols 16-29 via out[r,A-c]=out[r,c] for c in 2-15.
       Corner cells (rows 0-1, cols 0-1) are fully free.
    2. Place 2-3 maroon (9) hole blobs on the output to create the input.
       Each blob is a rectangle or L-shape (union of 2 axis-aligned rectangles).
       Validate repairability constraint - for each hole cell, at least one
       reflection orbit mate (or quarter-turn mate for edge-band cells) must NOT be a hole.
       Reject or shrink blobs that violate this.
    3. Return the grid with holes as the input.
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

